---
import Exhibit from "../components/Exhibit.astro";
import ChapterOpener from "../components/ChapterOpener.astro";
import Term from "../components/Term.astro";
import PractitionerNotes from "../components/PractitionerNotes.astro";
import Definitions from "../components/Definitions.astro";
import Pager from "../components/Pager.astro";
import AciPlayground from "../islands/AciPlayground.svelte";
import { load, pct, type Frame } from "../lib/data";
import { pack } from "../lib/pack";

const meta = load("meta");
const metrics = load("metrics");
const golden = load("golden");
const cal = load<Frame>("calibration");
const vol = load<Frame>("vol");
const vm = metrics.vol;
const firstYear = meta.splits.test.start.slice(0, 4);
const level = 1 - meta.alpha;
const t = vm.mondrian.by_tercile;
---
<section class="chapter" id="adapt" aria-labelledby="adapt-h">
  <ChapterOpener n="5" title="When markets shift" minutes={3} question="Why do guarantees break, and how do you repair them?" hid="adapt-h" />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The fix is a feedback loop. <Term id="aci" title="Adaptive Conformal Inference (Gibbs and Candès, 2021).">Adaptive conformal inference</Term>
      keeps score as it goes. Each time the range misses, it widens the next ranges a little; each time
      it holds, it narrows them a little. The size of that nudge is the step size γ.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pay-off is a different kind of guarantee. Over a long run the miss rate comes back to the
      promise for any sequence of markets, calm or not, with no assumption that the future looks like the past.
      Below, the whole test period is re-run in your browser at the promise and step size you choose.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="exhibit-wide">
    <Exhibit
      letter="F"
      title={`${firstYear} again, with a range that learns from its misses`}
      source={`Adaptive conformal inference on the same forecast and calibration scores as Exhibit E. Outcomes are revealed ${golden.feedback_lag} trading days late, as a 5-day target would be in practice. Computed in your browser and checked against the Python reference values.`}
      id="exhibit-f"
    >
      <AciPlayground
        client:visible
        data={pack(cal, vol)}
        lag={golden.feedback_lag}
        floorRatio={golden.floor_ratio}
        defaultGamma={golden.gamma}
        focusYear={firstYear}
      />
      <table slot="table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Year, at the {pct(level, 0)} promise</th><th scope="col">Adaptive held</th><th scope="col">Fixed held</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {Object.entries(vm.aci.by_year).map(([yr, v]) => (
            <tr><td>{yr}</td><td>{pct(v as number)}</td><td>{pct(vm.split_absolute.by_year[yr])}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Exhibit>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      At {pct(level, 0)} and the study's step size, the adaptive range held on {pct(vm.aci.by_year[firstYear])}
      of days in {firstYear} and {pct(vm.aci.coverage)} overall. The working level shows the
      mechanism: it fell in March {firstYear} as misses piled up, which widened the range in time
      for the rest of the year. Push γ up and it reacts faster but jitters; push it down and it
      behaves like the textbook method.
    </p>
    <p>
      I tested two other repairs. DtACI runs several step sizes at once and follows whichever has
      done best: {pct(vm.dtaci.coverage)} overall, the most even across calm and stormy markets.
      Mondrian conformal keeps separate cut-offs for calm, normal and stormy regimes:
      {" "}{pct(vm.mondrian.coverage)} overall, {pct(t.high)} in stormy markets but {pct(t.low)} in calm ones.
    </p>
  </div>

  <PractitionerNotes>
    <p>
      α<sub>t+1</sub> = α<sub>t</sub> + γ(α − err<sub>t</sub>), with err revealed after a
      {" "}{golden.feedback_lag}-day lag; q̂<sub>t</sub> is the conformal quantile at max(α<sub>t</sub>,
      {" "}{golden.floor_ratio}α, 1/(n+1)) over all scores seen so far. The floor keeps ranges finite at extreme
      levels. Long-run bound: |mean err − α| ≤ (max(α₁, 1−α₁) + γ)/(γT). Worst 63-day coverage:
      ACI {pct(vm.aci.worst_rolling_coverage)}, split {pct(vm.split_absolute.worst_rolling_coverage)}.
      DtACI scores its experts on the levels actually issued, so delayed feedback does not
      bias the expert weights.
    </p>
  </PractitionerNotes>

  <Definitions
    items={[
      { id: "aci", term: "ACI", def: "Adaptive conformal inference: after each miss widen, after each hit narrow, by a step γ." },
    ]}
  />
  <Pager id="adapt" />
</section>
